4. The Device's Claimed Identity

One of the first findings was that the device does not consistently present the same identity to every observer.

In its default USB configuration, it presents identifiers corresponding to a Google Pixel:

idVendor       0x18d1
iManufacturer  "Google"
iProduct       "Pixel"

The underlying hardware, however, is a Qualcomm SDM662-based automotive module.

When the relevant factory configuration is changed, the device instead presents a Qualcomm identity corresponding to the underlying platform.

This matters because operating systems, endpoint-security products, USB-control systems, and other security tools frequently make decisions based upon device identity.

A device that identifies itself as a Google Pixel is treated differently from an unidentified automotive IoT device.

The firmware therefore contains a mechanism by which the same physical hardware can present materially different identities.

The investigation also identified multiple brand profiles within the same firmware.

The retail brand was therefore not sufficient to identify the underlying software platform.
